# Atlas {#sec-atlas}

Every entry below follows one fixed template — a provenance class, a citable
canonical introduction, the domain the form organises, a gloss of one or two
sentences, and a reproduction generated from data or a faithful model instance.
The set is meant to read as a catalogue, not an anthology: the shape repeats so
that the differences between forms, not the differences between write-ups, do
the work. Each entry carries a *citable canonical introduction* rather than an
asserted origin, and where the earliest instance and the cited introduction are
different documents both are named.

| Class | Means |
|-------|-------|
| **Named first** | A specific, attributable earliest instance. |
| **Canonical reference** | The publication the field cites as the introduction, which may post-date the first. |
| **Shared genre idiom** | A form with no single inventor; emerged across multiple hands. |
| **Uncertain attribution** | Contested or unverified origin; flagged, not resolved. |

The order is a teaching order, not a chronology and not a subject index. It runs
from the raw perceptual channels a single mark can exploit (Part A), through the
Gestalt principles that let many marks group into structure (Part B), to the
dynamic and uncertain territory beyond the static frame (Part C). Entries keep a
running number from 1 to 103 and sit under unit headings that carry the
argument. The AIATSIS map of Indigenous Australia stands before them all.

Every figure is redrawn from data or a reproduced model instance, with one
deliberate exception: the AIATSIS map that opens the book is reproduced with
attribution. Every other historical image is described in prose and
cited. Reproductions use representative data where the original series is not
to hand — enough to show the form the field made canonical, never passed off as
the original measurement.

# Prelude — Acknowledgement of Country {#sec-prelude}

## AIATSIS Map of Indigenous Australia {#sec-aiatsis}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@horton1996aiatsis] \
> **Domain:** country, language and nation groups across the Australian continent

This book was written on Aboriginal land. David Horton's map for the Australian
Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Studies renders the general
locations of more than 250 language, social and nation groups across the
continent — an attempt to show, on one sheet, that the land was never empty
[@horton1996aiatsis]. Its boundaries are deliberately soft: they are a general
guide, contested in places, and explicitly not for use in native-title claims.
The map is reproduced below with attribution; the problem of drawing a curved
continent onto a flat sheet returns as a formal question at entry 81.

![The AIATSIS map of Indigenous Australia. Aboriginal Australia Wall Map, David R Horton (creator), © Aboriginal Studies Press, AIATSIS and Auslig/Sinclair, Knight, Merz, 1996. The map indicates only the general location of larger groupings of people; boundaries are not intended to be exact, and it is not suitable for use in native title or other land claims.](images/aiatsis-map.jpg){#fig-aiatsis}

# Day One — cold open {#sec-dayone}

Two figures open the book because they make its case before any theory does.
Anscombe's quartet (entry 18) holds four datasets with the same mean, variance,
correlation and regression line, and four completely different shapes — the
argument for why a summary statistic is not a substitute for a picture. John
Snow's cholera map (entry 78) is the consequence: a picture that was an
argument, and that argument changed what a city did about its water. Both live
in their proper units below; they are named here because everything between them
is an elaboration of the same two claims — *look at the data*, and *a good
picture is a claim about cause*.

# Part A — Encoding & the pre-attentive channels {#sec-part-a}

The pre-attentive channels — position, length, size, colour, orientation — are read before attention lands, and each is read at a sharply different precision. This part walks them one at a time, each illustrated by the canonical figure that made the channel famous.

## A0. The channels themselves {#sec-unit-a0}

*After Bertin, Sémiologie graphique (1967).*

### 1 — Bertin's visual-variables chart {#sec-bertin}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@bertin1967semiologie] \
> **Domain:** the grammar of marks

Every mark on a plane carries information through a fixed set of retinal variables — position, size, value, texture, colour, orientation, shape — and Bertin was the first to name them as a system and rank their perceptual power [@bertin1967semiologie]. The chart holds every variable constant but one, so each row shows a single channel varying in isolation.

::: {.cell execution_count=1}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![The retinal variables, each varied in isolation along a row.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-bertin-output-1.pdf){#fig-bertin}
:::
:::


## A1. Magnitude — length, size, and the biased area channel {#sec-unit-a1}

*After Cleveland & McGill, "Graphical Perception" (1984).*

### 2 — Mohs hardness scale {#sec-mohs}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@mohs1825treatise] \
> **Domain:** rank as position

Mohs ordered ten reference minerals so that each scratches the one below, giving a clean ordinal ladder from talc to diamond [@mohs1825treatise]. Placed against absolute hardness the ladder betrays itself — the rungs are evenly spaced by rank yet the Vickers numbers explode near the top, so rank is not magnitude.

::: {.cell execution_count=2}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Mohs rank (evenly spaced) against absolute Vickers hardness (wildly nonlinear).](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-mohs-output-1.pdf){#fig-mohs}
:::
:::


### 3 — Population pyramids {#sec-population-pyramid}

> **Class:** uncertain attribution \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@walker1874statistical] \
> **Domain:** a distribution split by category

Back-to-back horizontal bars set male against female across age bands, so the shape of a population — youthful and broad, or ageing and top-heavy — is read at a glance. The form appears in Francis Walker's census atlas [@walker1874statistical], though who drew the first such pyramid is not settled.

::: {.cell execution_count=3}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Back-to-back age–sex bars: male left, female right, counts tapering with age.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-population-pyramid-output-1.pdf){#fig-population-pyramid}
:::
:::


### 4 — Cosmic abundance of the elements {#sec-abundance}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@suess1956abundances] \
> **Domain:** the odd–even sawtooth

Plotting abundance against atomic number on a log scale shows two facts at once: a steep overall decline, and a persistent sawtooth in which even-Z elements sit above their odd neighbours — the Oddo–Harkins rule of nuclear stability. Suess and Urey compiled the reference abundance table that fixed the curve's shape [@suess1956abundances].

::: {.cell execution_count=4}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Cosmic abundance vs atomic number Z; even-Z elements ride above odd (Oddo–Harkins).](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-abundance-output-1.pdf){#fig-abundance}
:::
:::


### 5 — Box plot {#sec-boxplot}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@tukey1977eda] \
> **Domain:** robust summary read by length

The box plot compresses a batch to five robust numbers — median, quartiles, and whiskers — so spread and skew are read from length rather than computed. Tukey introduced it as part of exploratory data analysis [@tukey1977eda]; the runs shown here are representative of Michelson's 1879 speed-of-light experiment.

::: {.cell execution_count=5}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Box plots of five representative Michelson (1879) speed-of-light runs.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-boxplot-output-1.pdf){#fig-boxplot}
:::
:::


### 6 — Lorenz curve + Gini {#sec-lorenz}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@lorenz1905methods] \
> **Domain:** inequality as the area between curves

Cumulative income share plotted against cumulative population share bows below the 45° line of equality, and the area of that gap measures concentration. Lorenz proposed the curve to compare distributions of wealth [@lorenz1905methods]; twice the shaded area is the Gini coefficient.

::: {.cell execution_count=6}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Lorenz curve against the line of equality; shaded gap gives the Gini coefficient.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-lorenz-output-1.pdf){#fig-lorenz}
:::
:::


## A2. The single curve — distributions and profiles {#sec-unit-a2}

*After Tufte, The Visual Display of Quantitative Information (1983).*

### 7 — The bell curve (Gaussian) {#sec-gaussian}

> **Class:** named first + canonical reference (distinct documents) \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@gauss1809theoria] \
> **Named first:** [@demoivre1733approximatio]
> **Domain:** additive error

The normal curve is the shape additive errors pile into, and its bell is the single most recognisable profile in statistics. De Moivre derived it as the limiting form of the binomial [@demoivre1733approximatio]; Gauss gave it its canonical role as the law of observational error [@gauss1809theoria].

::: {.cell execution_count=7}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![The standard normal pdf, with ±1σ and ±2σ bands shaded.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-gaussian-output-1.pdf){#fig-gaussian}
:::
:::


### 8 — The log-normal {#sec-lognormal}

> **Class:** uncertain attribution \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@aitchison1957lognormal] \
> **Named first:** [@galton1879geometric]
> **Domain:** multiplicative processes

When effects multiply rather than add, the logarithm of the outcome is normal and the outcome itself is right-skewed with a long upper tail. Galton described the geometric mean's distribution [@galton1879geometric]; Aitchison and Brown gave the log-normal its canonical monograph treatment [@aitchison1957lognormal].

::: {.cell execution_count=8}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stderr}
```
/var/folders/mk/gq1zr39x11jg5qqz1cqbl99c0000gn/T/ipykernel_65279/3370549604.py:23: UserWarning: This figure includes Axes that are not compatible with tight_layout, so results might be incorrect.
  plt.tight_layout()
```
:::

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Right-skewed log-normal density; inset shows it normalised under the log transform.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-lognormal-output-2.pdf){#fig-lognormal}
:::
:::


### 9 — The power law / heavy tail {#sec-powerlaw}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@pareto1896cours] \
> **Domain:** the signature of complexity

A power law is a straight line on log-log axes, its slope the exponent, and its tail decays far more slowly than an exponential's. Pareto found the form in the distribution of incomes [@pareto1896cours]; it recurs across city sizes, word frequencies, and network degrees.

::: {.cell execution_count=9}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![A power law is straight on log-log axes; an exponential curves away.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-powerlaw-output-1.pdf){#fig-powerlaw}
:::
:::


### 10 — Species-abundance distribution {#sec-sad}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@fisher1943relation] \
> **Domain:** ecology's heavy tail

Binning species by abundance octave yields the hollow curve every community shares: a wall of rare species and a thin scatter of common ones. Fisher, Corbet, and Williams fitted the log-series that made the pattern quantitative [@fisher1943relation].

::: {.cell execution_count=10}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Species-abundance distribution: many rare species, few common (abundance octaves).](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-sad-output-1.pdf){#fig-sad}
:::
:::


### 11 — The S-curve / logistic {#sec-logistic}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@verhulst1838notice] \
> **Domain:** bounded growth

Growth that starts exponential but is checked by a ceiling traces an S, accelerating then saturating at the carrying capacity K. Verhulst named the logistic and wrote its closed-form solution [@verhulst1838notice].

::: {.cell execution_count=11}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![The logistic curve rising from near zero and saturating at carrying capacity K.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-logistic-output-1.pdf){#fig-logistic}
:::
:::


### 12 — Violin plot {#sec-violin}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@hintze1998violin] \
> **Domain:** a density shape mirrored

The violin mirrors a kernel density estimate about its axis, so multimodality and skew that a box plot hides become visible in the outline. Hintze and Nelson introduced the form and its name [@hintze1998violin].

::: {.cell execution_count=12}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Violin plots of four groups; the mirrored density reveals bimodal shape.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-violin-output-1.pdf){#fig-violin}
:::
:::


### 13 — Reaction energy-coordinate diagram {#sec-energy-coordinate}

> **Class:** shared genre idiom \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@evans1935applications] \
> **Domain:** an energy landscape

Potential energy plotted against a reaction coordinate shows reactants and products as wells separated by a transition-state hump whose height is the activation energy. Evans and Polanyi gave the profile its canonical thermodynamic reading [@evans1935applications]; the barrier sets the rate, the well depth difference sets the equilibrium.

::: {.cell execution_count=13}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Energy vs reaction coordinate: reactant well, activation hump, lower product well.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-energy-coordinate-output-1.pdf){#fig-energy-coordinate}
:::
:::


## A3. Position — relating two variables {#sec-unit-a3}

*Cleveland, The Elements of Graphing Data (1985), ranks position along a common scale as the channel the eye decodes most accurately — so relating two variables begins with points in a plane.*

### 14 — Hertzsprung–Russell diagram {#sec-hr}

> **Class:** named first + canonical reference (distinct documents) \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@russell1914relations] \
> **Named first:** [@hertzsprung1911uber]
> **Domain:** stellar evolution read as a sequence, not a snapshot

Plotting luminosity against temperature turns the whole stellar population into a shape — a dense main sequence running diagonally, a giant clump, a scatter of white dwarfs — that later reads as an evolutionary track. Hertzsprung tabulated the luminosity–colour relation first [@hertzsprung1911uber]; Russell drew the diagram the field now cites [@russell1914relations].

::: {.cell execution_count=14}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Synthetic Hertzsprung–Russell diagram: luminosity against temperature, hot stars to the left, brightness upward.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-hr-output-1.pdf){#fig-hr}
:::
:::


### 15 — Hubble diagram {#sec-hubble}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@hubble1929relation] \
> **Domain:** the expansion of the universe as a straight line

A galaxy's recession velocity rises in proportion to its distance, and the slope of that line is the expansion rate itself. Hubble's 1929 plot fixed the relation with a scatter of a few dozen galaxies through a line near the origin [@hubble1929relation].

::: {.cell execution_count=15}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Recession velocity against distance at 1929 scale, with a linear fit through the origin.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-hubble-output-1.pdf){#fig-hubble}
:::
:::


### 16 — Kleiber's law {#sec-kleiber}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@kleiber1932body] \
> **Domain:** metabolic rate against body mass across the tree of life

On log–log axes the metabolic rates of animals spanning six orders of magnitude in mass fall on a single line of slope three-quarters. Kleiber measured the exponent and named the regularity that carries his name [@kleiber1932body].

::: {.cell execution_count=16}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Metabolic rate against body mass, log–log, on a line of slope 3/4.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-kleiber-output-1.pdf){#fig-kleiber}
:::
:::


### 17 — Zipf's law of city sizes {#sec-zipf}

> **Class:** named first + canonical reference (distinct documents) \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@zipf1949human] \
> **Named first:** [@auerbach1913gesetz]
> **Domain:** rank against size in a heavy-tailed population

Rank a country's cities from largest down and plot size against rank on log–log axes: the points follow a straight line of slope near $-1$, so the second city is roughly half the first. Auerbach first noticed the rank–size regularity [@auerbach1913gesetz]; Zipf made it a general law of human systems [@zipf1949human].

::: {.cell execution_count=17}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![City population against rank, log–log, on a line of slope near −1.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-zipf-output-1.pdf){#fig-zipf}
:::
:::


### 18 — Anscombe's quartet {#sec-anscombe}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@anscombe1973graphs] \
> **Domain:** the argument for plotting the data at all

Four datasets share the same mean, variance, correlation and fitted line $y = 3 + 0.5x$, yet a linear trend, a curve, an outlier-bent line and a single leverage point could not look less alike. This is the book's Day-One cold open — Anscombe built the quartet precisely to show that a summary statistic is no substitute for the picture [@anscombe1973graphs].

::: {.cell execution_count=18}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Anscombe's four datasets, each with the identical fit y = 3 + 0.5x.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-anscombe-output-1.pdf){#fig-anscombe}
:::
:::


### 19 — P–T phase diagram {#sec-phase-diagram}

> **Class:** canonical reference \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@gibbs1876equilibrium] \
> **Domain:** the state space of matter

Pressure against temperature carves the plane into solid, liquid and gas, with sublimation, fusion and vaporisation curves meeting at a triple point and the liquid–gas line ending abruptly at a critical point. Gibbs's phase rule gave the diagram its coordinates and its constraints [@gibbs1876equilibrium].

::: {.cell execution_count=19}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Schematic pressure–temperature phase diagram with triple and critical points.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-phase-diagram-output-1.pdf){#fig-phase-diagram}
:::
:::


### 20 — Ramachandran plot {#sec-ramachandran}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@ramachandran1963stereochemistry] \
> **Domain:** the allowed backbone conformations of a protein

The two backbone dihedral angles $\varphi$ and $\psi$ span a torus, and steric clashes forbid most of it — leaving tight clusters at the $\alpha$-helix and $\beta$-sheet conformations. Ramachandran computed the allowed regions from hard-sphere contacts and gave the plot its name [@ramachandran1963stereochemistry].

::: {.cell execution_count=20}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Backbone dihedral angles φ against ψ, clustered in the allowed regions.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-ramachandran-output-1.pdf){#fig-ramachandran}
:::
:::


## A4. The line over time — trends {#sec-unit-a4}

*Playfair's time-series line, as Tufte reads it, turns a column of dated numbers into a single continuous trend the eye follows without effort.*

### 21 — Keeling curve {#sec-keeling}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@keeling1960concentration] \
> **Domain:** the line that moved climate policy

Atmospheric CO$_2$ climbs from about 315 ppm in 1958 toward 420 ppm, with an annual sawtooth from the breathing of Northern-Hemisphere forests riding the upward trend. Keeling's continuous Mauna Loa record established both the rise and its seasonal signature [@keeling1960concentration].

::: {.cell execution_count=21}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Atmospheric CO2 rising with an annual sawtooth (representative shape).](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-keeling-output-1.pdf){#fig-keeling}
:::
:::


### 22 — Hockey stick {#sec-hockeystick}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@mann1998global] \
> **Domain:** a millennium of reconstructed temperature

A temperature anomaly reconstructed from proxies stays roughly flat for nine centuries, then bends sharply upward in the twentieth — the blade of the stick. Mann, Bradley and Hughes assembled the multiproxy reconstruction that fixed the shape and its uncertainty band [@mann1998global].

::: {.cell execution_count=22}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Reconstructed temperature anomaly, flat then sharply rising, with an uncertainty band.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-hockeystick-output-1.pdf){#fig-hockeystick}
:::
:::


### 23 — Sea-ice annual-cycle graph {#sec-seaice}

> **Class:** canonical reference \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@fetterer2017seaice] \
> **Domain:** one calendar year, a line per year

Drawing sea-ice extent month by month, one line per year, stacks the seasonal max–min cycle and lets a downward drift across decades show as later lines sitting lower. The National Snow and Ice Data Center's sea-ice index is the reference series for this now-standard spaghetti plot [@fetterer2017seaice].

::: {.cell execution_count=23}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Monthly sea-ice extent, one line per year, coloured by year (representative shape).](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-seaice-output-1.pdf){#fig-seaice}
:::
:::


### 24 — Kyoto cherry-blossom bloom dates {#sec-cherry}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@aono2008phenological] \
> **Domain:** twelve centuries of phenology

Kyoto's court diaries record the day of peak cherry bloom back to the ninth century, giving a noisy line that plunges to markedly earlier dates in the warming modern era. Aono and Kazui reconstructed the full-flowering series and the March temperatures behind it [@aono2008phenological].

::: {.cell execution_count=24}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Kyoto full-bloom day of year, 800–2020, with a rolling mean (representative shape).](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-cherry-output-1.pdf){#fig-cherry}
:::
:::


### 25 — Candlestick / financial series {#sec-candlestick}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@nison1991candlestick] \
> **Domain:** four prices per session in one dense mark

Each session becomes a candle: a body from open to close, thin wicks to the high and low, coloured by whether the price rose or fell — four numbers per bar with almost no ink. Nison brought the Japanese rice-trading technique to Western markets and named it for the West [@nison1991candlestick].

::: {.cell execution_count=25}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![OHLC candlesticks for thirty synthetic sessions; up sessions green, down red.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-candlestick-output-1.pdf){#fig-candlestick}
:::
:::


### 26 — Culturomics / Google Books Ngram {#sec-ngram}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@michel2011quantitative] \
> **Domain:** a word's frequency across centuries

Relative frequency against year traces the life of a word — one term rising as its rival falls, their curves crossing where usage changed hands. Michel and colleagues built the culturomic corpus that made these trajectories a quantitative object [@michel2011quantitative].

::: {.cell execution_count=26}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Relative frequency of three competing terms crossing over (synthetic).](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-ngram-output-1.pdf){#fig-ngram}
:::
:::


### 27 — LIGO gravitational-wave chirp {#sec-ligo}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@abbott2016observation] \
> **Domain:** a signal in time

The strain from two merging black holes sweeps up in frequency and amplitude — the chirp — then rings down in a few cycles once the horizons fuse. The LIGO collaboration's GW150914 detection was the first direct observation of the waveform [@abbott2016observation].

::: {.cell execution_count=27}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Synthetic gravitational-wave chirp: strain against time, sweeping up then ringing down.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-ligo-output-1.pdf){#fig-ligo}
:::
:::


### 28 — Exoplanet transit & radial-velocity curves {#sec-exoplanet}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@charbonneau2000detection] \
> **Domain:** the dimming and the wobble

A transiting planet cuts a flat-bottomed dip in the star's light while its gravity pulls the star through a radial-velocity sinusoid — two independent curves for one orbit. Charbonneau and colleagues measured the first transit of a known exoplanet, joining the photometric dip to the spectroscopic wobble [@charbonneau2000detection].

::: {.cell execution_count=28}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Stacked transit light curve and radial-velocity curve against orbital phase.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-exoplanet-output-1.pdf){#fig-exoplanet}
:::
:::


### 29 — Yield curve {#sec-yieldcurve}

> **Class:** shared genre idiom \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@macaulay1938movements] \
> **Domain:** a cross-sectional curve read as a signal

Yield against maturity is normally an upward slope; when it flattens or inverts — short rates above long — the shape itself is read as a recession signal. Macaulay's study of interest-rate movements gave the term-structure curve its analytical footing [@macaulay1938movements].

::: {.cell execution_count=29}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Yield against maturity for a normal and an inverted date.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-yieldcurve-output-1.pdf){#fig-yieldcurve}
:::
:::


## A5. Colour — luminance, hue, the wheel, and colour-blindness {#sec-unit-a5}

*Ware, Information Visualization (2004), treats colour as three separable channels — luminance, hue, saturation — each with its own perceptual budget and its own failure under colour-blindness.*

### 30 — Colour wheel {#sec-colourwheel}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@newton1704opticks] \
> **Domain:** the organisation of colour into a closed ring

Bending the spectrum into a circle joins violet back to red and turns hue into an angle — the first diagram to treat colour as a cyclic quantity. Newton drew the wheel in the *Opticks* [@newton1704opticks]; Dalton's 1798 account of his own colour-blindness stands as the conscience of every palette choice that follows.

::: {.cell execution_count=30}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![A hue wheel: wedges around a circle spanning the spectrum.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-colourwheel-output-1.pdf){#fig-colourwheel}
:::
:::


### 31 — Climate stripes {#sec-climatestripes}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@hawkins2018warming] \
> **Domain:** temperature as pure colour, axes removed

Stripping every axis and label leaves one coloured band per year on a blue-to-red diverging scale — warming shown as colour alone, legible with no training. Hawkins designed the warming-stripes graphic for exactly that reach [@hawkins2018warming].

::: {.cell execution_count=31}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![One coloured stripe per year on a diverging scale; no axes.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-climatestripes-output-1.pdf){#fig-climatestripes}
:::
:::


### 32 — CMB temperature map {#sec-cmb-map}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@smoot1992structure] \
> **Domain:** the oldest light as a field of fluctuations

Tiny temperature variations across the microwave sky — parts in a hundred thousand — become a field of hot and cold blotches on a diverging colour scale. COBE's DMR, reported by Smoot and colleagues, first resolved the anisotropy [@smoot1992structure].

::: {.cell execution_count=32}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![A smoothed Gaussian random field standing for CMB temperature anisotropy.](atlas_files/figure-pdf/fig-cmb-map-output-1.pdf){#fig-cmb-map}
:::
:::


### 33 — Ozone hole {#sec-ozone}

> **Class:** named first \
> **Canonical introduction:** [@farman1985large] \
> **Domain:** the Antarctic false-colour map

Total-column ozone mapped over the pole shows a deep central minimum — the hole — rendered in false colour so the depletion reads instantly. Farman, Gardiner and Shanklin's ground measurements first reported the springtime Antarctic loss [@farman1985large].

::: {.cell execution_count=33}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
